+++ b/CONTROL_HELPERS.md
@@ -56,3 +56,32 @@ effective `configuration` after applying
 true, as required by the current Media specification; Media2 also persists
 encoder changes. Rejected changes must leave the camera unchanged. Setters
 remain unavailable when no encoder helper is configured.
+
+## Imaging operations
+
+An `imaging_helper` enables the Imaging service at `/onvif/imaging_service`
+and advertises it through Device GetServices and GetCapabilities. Install a
+CGI wrapper selecting that service, as for the existing media endpoints.
+All operations receive `token: "VideoSourceToken"`, the sole camera source.
+
+`get` returns `revision` and a `settings` object. `options` returns `revision`
+and an `options` object. Supported top-level keys, in either object, are
+`brightness`, `colour_saturation`, `contrast`, `exposure`, `sharpness` and
+`white_balance`. Omit controls the camera does not support.
+
+Exposure contains `mode` and optional `min_exposure_time`, `max_exposure_time`,
+`min_gain`, `max_gain`, `min_iris`, `max_iris`, `exposure_time`, `gain`, `iris`.
+Exposure times are microseconds; gain and iris attenuation are dB. White
+balance contains `mode` and optional unitless `cr_gain` (red) and `cb_gain`
+(blue). Settings use a single `AUTO` or `MANUAL` mode and numeric values.
+Options use an array of supported modes and numeric `{min, max}` ranges.
+The adapter uses the ONVIF schema's `YrGain`/`YbGain` names for white-balance
+options, and `CrGain`/`CbGain` for settings.
+
+`set` receives `settings`, a partial object of changes, and the caller's
+`force_persistence` boolean, false when omitted. Each exposure/white-balance
+object includes its mode. Apply the whole request as one transaction. Return
+`revision` and the effective `settings` after application. The helper must
+reject unsupported controls or values, honour persistence and preserve
+unchanged controls. Unknown XML controls are rejected rather than ignored.
+Focus, presets, IR-cut, WDR and exposure masks are outside this interface.
